 the model in different contexts, and the use of pre-defined rules for the definition of the graph limits the model’s universal applicability. More than half of the papers employ a dynamic graph structure, which adapts to the varying temporal patterns observed in the data. The two most commonly utilized loss functions are the MSE and the MAE. Since the graph structure is typically learned by the models themselves, many papers include a regularization term in the loss function for the optimization of the graph structure (e.g., Refs. [193, 195, 92, 43]). The majority of the papers specify the language and libraries used for the code, namely Python with PyTorch, PyTorch Geometric [68], and Torch Spatiotemporal [48]. However, only slightly less than half of the papers provide a link to the source code. The links to the code repositories are listed in Tab. 34.
